  lastUpdated="2026-07-09"
  faqs={FAQS}
  sources={SOURCES}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Learn', url: `${SITE_URL}/learn/` },
    { name: 'Support a nonprofit without spending extra', url: `${SITE_URL}/learn/support-a-nonprofit-without-spending-extra/` },
  ]}
>
  <h2>Give through spending you already do</h2>
  <p>
    The easiest way to support a cause without a new line item in your budget is to attach the giving to purchases
    you’d make anyway. Three approaches, from simplest to most impactful:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shopping portals</strong> (ShopRaise, Givebacks, iGive) — a percentage of online purchases at partner retailers goes to your nonprofit, paid by the retailer.</li>
    <li><strong>Gift-card programs</strong> (RaiseRight) — buy brand gift cards you’d use anyway; a percentage funds your organization.</li>
    <li><strong>Local-first models</strong> (Good Circles) — shop local businesses; the price is already ~10% lower, and 10% of the merchant’s profit funds the nonprofit you chose.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why local-first gives the most — and costs you the least</h2>
  <p>
    Most shop-and-give tools ask you to give a small slice of a purchase at no cost. A local-first model does that
    <em>and</em> puts money back in your pocket: on Good Circles, every <strong>$100</strong> spent locally returns
    about <strong>{dollars(PER_100.shopperSaves)}</strong> to you, sends about <strong>{dollars(PER_100.nonprofit)}</strong>
    to a nonprofit you choose, and keeps roughly <strong>${PER_100.localRecirculation}</strong> circulating in your own
    community. Same spending — supporting a cause, your budget, and your local economy at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Not sure which nonprofit? See <a href="/learn/how-to-choose-a-local-nonprofit/">how to choose a local nonprofit</a>,
    or explore <a href="/learn/passive-nonprofit-funding/">every honest passive-funding option</a>.
  </p>
</AnswerPage>
